  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "n1",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 30,
      "color": "blue",
      "anchorStates": [],
      "anchorTransitions": [],
      "text": "A 4-tape ALU. Tape 1 holds operand A, tape 2 operand B (both least-significant bit first), tape 3 holds the opcode, tape 4 receives the result. One decode step reads the opcode and jumps to the circuit for it; from there every operation is a single lockstep sweep of four heads."
    },
    {
      "id": "n2",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 250,
      "color": "green",
      "anchorStates": [],
      "anchorTransitions": [],
      "text": "Input format: A,B,op,ε — one segment per tape. Opcodes: + add, & and, | or, ^ xor, ~ not. Try 1101,0110,+,ε (11+6=17)."
    },
    {
      "id": "n3",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 470,
      "color": "amber",
      "anchorStates": [],
      "anchorTransitions": [],
      "text": "The opcode head never moves, so tape 3 stays readable for the whole run and every rule names the operation it belongs to. That is why there is no separate wiring per instruction: the opcode IS the selector, exactly as it is in hardware."
    },
    {
      "id": "n4",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 700,
      "color": "purple",
      "anchorStates": [],
      "anchorTransitions": [],
      "text": "~ is unary: it reads tape 2 as blank, so supplying a second operand leaves no matching rule and the word is refused. Add is the only path that needs memory — one bit of it, the carry, which is the whole difference between the two add states."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "title": "4-tape ALU — add, and, or, xor, not",
    "blurb": "An arithmetic logic unit as a Turing machine: operands on tapes 1 and 2, the opcode on tape 3, the result on tape 4. Decode once, then one synchronized sweep per operation — the carry bit is the only state the machine ever needs.",
